# Mutation plan for the three job reads (GET /api/v4/jobs, /jobs/{job_id}, /jobs/type/{job_type}).
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/jobs.plan
#
# The decisions worth mutating here are almost all about *which* empty answer a route gives and
# *which* status a refusal carries — neither of which any type can protect. The nil-versus-empty
# flag is a bare `bool` at three call sites, so flipping each one is a separate mutation.
#
# Four mutations were re-pointed after a first run reported them SURVIVED, and each survivor was a
# finding about the fixtures rather than about the code:
#
#   * the two `page_in_memory` lines run under `unit`. Their branch is reached only by the
#     `team_id`/`policy_id` filters, which need an access-control job — and creating one needs a
#     licence, so no HTTP request on Team Edition reaches the sort at all. The unit tests do.
#   * `store-data-filter-*` run under `store`, against `job_data_filter`, for the same reason
#     seen from the other end: with zero matching rows in `Jobs`, a broken predicate and a correct
#     one both return `[]`. That test plants the rows.
#   * `app-data-retention-uses-read-jobs` stayed on `api` and the *suite* was fixed instead: every
#     stock role granting `read_jobs` also grants `read_data_retention_job`, so a planted role
#     holding only the first is what separates them.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.

api-getjobs-empty-is-a-list	crates/mm-api/src/jobs.rs	    if jobs.is_empty() && nil_when_empty {	    if false {	api	jobs
api-bytype-empty-is-null	crates/mm-api/src/jobs.rs	    Ok(json_ok(encode_jobs(&jobs, false)?))	    Ok(json_ok(encode_jobs(&jobs, true)?))	api	jobs
